#!/bin/bash
# ops-claude-failover (cron, every minute, smd-ops and every client workspace):
# move a Claude session that is stuck at its account's usage limit onto the
# Captain's other account, resuming the same conversation, with no person typing.
#
# Why: on 2026-11-08 the A&P workspace's Claude account hit its weekly limit and
# every window sat at Claude's limit prompt (stop / wait / switch to usage
# credits) until the reset, while the Captain's second account sat unused.
#
# How (logic, not a model):
#   * The claude shim registers every interactive session it runs inside tmux
#     in ~/state/claude-panes/pane-<n>: its own pid, the tmux pane, the account,
#     the session id and the Machine's account order. Only sessions on an
#     account token are registered; with no token this script finds nothing.
#   * Each run reads the bottom of each registered pane. Claude's limit message
#     is "You've hit your <session|weekly|Opus|...> limit · resets <when>"
#     (Claude Code docs, errors page, 2026-11-08). Spend, budget and fast-mode
#     limits are not account limits and are ignored.
#   * Seen once is not enough: a session can print those words while working
#     (an agent discussing this very script). Only a pane showing the message on
#     two runs in a row with an unchanged screen is stuck. Then the account is
#     marked exhausted until its stated reset (ops-claude-accounts mark), and if
#     another account is available the pane gets a .restart file and its Claude
#     is sent TERM; the shim resumes the conversation on the next account.
#   * Every account exhausted: no restart (it would land on a spent account),
#     and team@ is paged ONCE with the reset times, through the alert path this
#     Machine already has: smd-ops emails through Resend as ops-triage does; a
#     workspace fails its own healthchecks.io check (OPS_HC_URL), which emails.
#     When the earliest reset passes, a still-stuck pane is restarted onto it.
# Test seams: OPS_FAILOVER_DRY=1 prints the actions instead of taking them.
set -uo pipefail
S="$HOME/state"; P="$S/claude-panes"
mkdir -p "$P" "$HOME/logs"
# Resolve a symlink first: ~/bin/<name> may point here (ops-home-bin-sync), and
# the helpers beside this script live in the image directory, not ~/bin.
here="$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")" && pwd)"
acct="$here/ops-claude-accounts"
dry="${OPS_FAILOVER_DRY:-}"
now=$(date +%s)
log() { echo "$(date -u +%FT%TZ) $*"; }

# One run at a time (a slow tmux must not stack runs). A directory, not a
# ~/locks/*.lock file: those keep a workspace awake (ops-idle-stop).
lock="$S/claude-failover.lockdir"
if ! mkdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null; then
  [ -n "$(find "$lock" -maxdepth 0 -mmin +10 2>/dev/null)" ] && rm -rf "$lock"
  exit 0
fi
trap 'rmdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null' EXIT

page() {
  local msg="$1"
  if [ -n "$dry" ]; then echo "would page: $msg"; return 0; fi
  if [ -x "$HOME/bin/ss-env" ] && [ -f "$HOME/.infisical-ua" ]; then
    "$HOME/bin/ss-env" python3 - "$msg" <<'PY'
import json, os, socket, sys, urllib.request
msg = sys.argv[1]
req = urllib.request.Request(
    "https://api.resend.com/emails",
    data=json.dumps({"from": "SMD Services Ops <team@smd.services>", "to": ["team@smd.services"],
                     "subject": "[SMD Ops] Claude: every account at its usage limit on " + socket.gethostname(),
                     "text": msg + "\n\nSessions wait at the limit prompt and move on their own when the first account resets."}).encode(),
    headers={"Authorization": "Bearer " + os.environ["RESEND_API_KEY"], "Content-Type": "application/json",
             "User-Agent": "smd-ops-claude-failover/1"},
)
print("resend", urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30).status)
PY
    return $?
  fi
  # shellcheck disable=SC1091
  [ -f "$HOME/.ops-secrets" ] && . "$HOME/.ops-secrets"
  if [ -n "${OPS_HC_URL:-}" ]; then
    curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 --data-raw "$msg" "$OPS_HC_URL/fail" >/dev/null
    return $?
  fi
  log "no alert path on this Machine (no ss-env, no OPS_HC_URL): $msg"
  return 1
}

for f in "$P"/pane-*; do
  case "$f" in *.*|*'*') continue ;; esac
  [ -f "$f" ] || continue
  pid=$(sed -n 's/^pid=//p' "$f") pane=$(sed -n 's/^pane=//p' "$f")
  account=$(sed -n 's/^account=//p' "$f") accounts=$(sed -n 's/^accounts=//p' "$f")
  if [ -z "$pid" ] || ! kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then rm -f "$f" "$f".*; continue; fi
  [ "$account" = login ] && continue   # the stored /login: no other account to move to from here
  screen=$(tmux capture-pane -p -J -t "$pane" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 30) || continue
  hit=$(printf '%s\n' "$screen" | grep -E "You.ve hit your [A-Za-z0-9 .-]*limit" \
        | grep -v -i -E "spend|budget|fast limit" | grep -i "resets" | tail -1)
  if [ -z "$hit" ]; then rm -f "$f.seen"; continue; fi
  h=$(printf '%s' "$screen" | cksum | cut -d' ' -f1)
  prev=$(cat "$f.seen" 2>/dev/null || true)
  echo "$h" > "$f.seen"
  if [ "$prev" != "$h" ]; then log "pane $pane ($account): limit message on screen; confirming next run"; continue; fi

  # Stuck. Mark the account once per stuck screen (a stale screen must not
  # re-mark an account whose reset has already passed).
  if [ "$(cat "$f.handled" 2>/dev/null)" != "$h" ]; then
    log "pane $pane: $("$acct" mark "$account" "$hit")"
    echo "$h" > "$f.handled"
  fi
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  next=$("$acct" pick $accounts); rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    if summary=$("$acct" page-due $accounts); then
      log "pane $pane: every account exhausted; paging team@"
      # shellcheck disable=SC2086
      page "$summary" && "$acct" paged $accounts
    fi
    continue
  fi
  last=$(cat "$f.restarted" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
  if [ $((now - last)) -lt 300 ]; then log "pane $pane: restarted $((now - last))s ago; waiting"; continue; fi
  child=$(pgrep -P "$pid" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  log "pane $pane: moving the session from $account to $next"
  if [ -n "$dry" ]; then
    echo "would: restart pane $pane on $next (TERM claude ${child:-none})"
  else
    : > "$f.restart"
    [ -n "$child" ] && kill -TERM "$child"
  fi
  echo "$now" > "$f.restarted"
done
exit 0
